\title{Notes on formulating discrete-induced physics}
\tag{notes}
\tag{math}
\tag{draft}
\tag{agent-draft}
\tag{fdip}
\tag{discrete-physics}
\tag{lattice}
\tag{induced-gravity}
\author{utensil}
\date{2026-11-03}

\p{These are the accompanying mathematical notes for the experimental research
project FDIP. FDIP is read “F-dip,” for discrete-induced physics: physics built on
a discrete setting, with geometry, time and matter induced from it rather than
assumed. It is also a coined acronym for “Formulating Discrete-Induced Physics.”
A discrete substrate is what the hypothesis asks for, not a device for reaching a
continuum: a finite structure can be stated and computed in full, while a continuum
description commits to infinitely many values before any of them is measured.
Recovering a continuum from such a structure is then one demonstration of what the
theory can do.}

\p{Background independence is usually counted a virtue of a fundamental theory, and
Wilson argues that it is a hidden assumption rather than a property the Standard Model
possesses \citek{wilson2025background}. With Mach's principle in mind, the notes keep
their constructions compatible with a background-dependent physics rather than
requiring one: a declared background would index the objects of a construction, and
each construction is asked what such an indexing would add, what map would carry it
and whether it admits one, so compatibility is what gets tested.

The mathematics is worth developing because the environment may supply what nothing
internal fixes. On the bubble-universe picture — a mental model, not a claim made here
— a universe that lasts longer than an instant, and grows structured enough to
develop anything, needs a symmetry that admits sustained and rich physics, while a
symmetry that leaves parameters free admits many bubbles that share it, differing in
their initial conditions, with matter distributed differently inside and around them.
On that picture a physics would be settled by three things: a symmetry chosen for
what it can sustain, the conditions of one bubble at its creation, and the background
it sits in. What the mathematics has to make possible is saying which of the three a
parameter came from, and that is why the notes keep induced and supplied data apart
and why they want constructions that admit a background.}

\p{Physics motivates the notes, but the interest and the focus are the mathematics
that answers the challenges: no physical theory is claimed, and where the notes
include a falsifiable proposal for one, it is an example of applying the tools
developed here. The organizing
hypothesis is that operations which continuum analysis presents analytically can
be recast as algebra on finite-dimensional objects; the notes test it on small
cases before relying on it, and a proposed translation carries its carrier, its
map and the property proved of it.}

